<doc><paragraph heading='1'><text>L27. &quot;I&#x27;ll eat double to make up for you&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Thesis:</bold></text><text> it isn&#x27;t an argument; it&#x27;s an emotional reaction. Psychology calls it reactance: when people feel their freedom is threatened, they tend to do the opposite of what&#x27;s asked. The phrase says nothing about the animals; it says something about the discomfort of whoever says it. And the one who pays the &quot;make-up&quot; isn&#x27;t the vegan, it&#x27;s the animal.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Family:</bold></text><text> L. Attacks on veganism and vegans · </text><text><bold>Part:</bold></text><text> IND</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>1. Formulation</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Everyday version:</bold></text><text> &quot;I&#x27;ll eat some bacon in your honor. For every vegan, one more steak.&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Strongest version (steelman):</bold></text></paragraph><paragraph><text>There&#x27;s no strong version. The phrase is a provocation, not an argument. It can express:</text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Irritation</bold></text><text> at feeling judged.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Defense</bold></text><text> of one&#x27;s freedom of choice.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Humor</bold></text><text> to ease an uncomfortable conversation.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph><text>None of these readings offers a reason to eat animals.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>2. How it can show up</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Context</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Typical form</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;I&#x27;ll eat double to make up for you.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;I&#x27;ll eat some bacon in your honor.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;For every vegan, one more steak.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;For every vegan who doesn&#x27;t eat meat, I&#x27;ll eat double.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Social media</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;I ate three burgers just to annoy the vegans.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Disguised version</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;Vegans are annoying and think they are better.&quot; (which is L1)</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>3. Answer for children</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>Sometimes, when someone tells us not to do something, we feel like doing it even more, just to provoke them. But in this case, the one who gets hurt isn&#x27;t the person who asked, it&#x27;s the animals. Provoking doesn&#x27;t change what&#x27;s right.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>4. Short answer</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>That isn&#x27;t an argument; it&#x27;s a provocation. Psychology calls it reactance: when we feel our freedom is threatened, we tend to do the opposite. But the one who pays the &quot;make-up&quot; isn&#x27;t me; it&#x27;s the animals. If the goal is to annoy me, there are ways that don&#x27;t involve anyone&#x27;s death. And whenever you want to talk about the animals for real, I&#x27;m here.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Question to ask back:</bold></text><text> &quot;If it weren&#x27;t to provoke me, what do you think of what happens to the animals?&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>5. Full answer</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(a) Reconstruction and diagnosis</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The phrase has no argumentative structure:</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>What the phrase expresses</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>What it says about the animals</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Irritation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Nothing</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Defense of freedom</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Nothing</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Humor</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Nothing</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='3'><text>(b) Where the argument breaks</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>1. It isn&#x27;t an argument.</bold></text><text> There are no premises or conclusion about the moral question.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>2. It&#x27;s reactance.</bold></text><text> Feeling one&#x27;s freedom threatened produces a tendency to do the opposite of what&#x27;s suggested.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>3. The victim is the animal.</bold></text><text> The provocation is aimed at the vegan, but the harm falls on the animals.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>4. It reveals discomfort.</bold></text><text> Feeling the need to &quot;make up&quot; for it shows the question bothered the person, which is a starting point for conversation.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>5. Do-gooder derogation explains part of the reaction.</bold></text><text> Those who feel judged tend to react against the group that seems to judge them (L1).</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(c) Data and sources</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Source</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>What it says</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Note</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://faculty.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2011/12/Minson---Monin,-2011.pdf">Minson and Monin, 2012</link></text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Meat eaters disparage vegetarians when they anticipate being judged by them</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Helps explain the reaction</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4675534/">Brehm, theory of psychological reactance (1966)</link></text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Threats to freedom produce a motivation to restore it, often by doing the opposite</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Academic Press; the link is to an open-access review by Steindl and colleagues (2015)</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='3'><text>(d) The strongest counter-reply: it&#x27;s just a joke</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The most serious version says the phrase is only humor, with no real intention to eat more meat, and that taking it seriously shows a lack of humor.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(e) Why the counter-reply doesn&#x27;t reach the conclusion</text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>If it&#x27;s a joke, it isn&#x27;t an argument.</bold></text><text> And the moral question is still unanswered.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Humor can close the conversation.</bold></text><text> Often, that&#x27;s exactly what it&#x27;s used for.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>The joke has a real target.</bold></text><text> The animals.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph heading='3'><text>(f) Back to the moral question</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The provocation is a way of not talking. But the question remains: setting aside irritation with vegans, is it fair to make animals suffer and die when we don&#x27;t need to?</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>6. Connections</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Direction</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Code</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Why it appears</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>L1 &quot;Vegans are annoying and think they are better&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Irritation becomes provocation</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>L6 &quot;Meat is for real men&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>I&#x27;ll eat double</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes after</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>E1 &quot;It&#x27;s my personal choice; don&#x27;t impose your morals&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>The provocation becomes a defense of freedom</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes after</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>J5 &quot;Meat tastes good&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>The provocation reveals the pleasure</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>7. Reasoning errors</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Level</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Error</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>How it appears in L27</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Logical</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Absence of argument</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Provocation in place of reason</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Moral</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Harm to third parties as a response</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>The animal pays for the irritation with the vegan</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Moral</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Reactance</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Freedom becomes a reason to do the opposite</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>8. Contradictions</text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Says the choice is personal.</bold></text><text> And makes it a response to someone else.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Wouldn&#x27;t eat more meat to annoy other groups.</bold></text><text> Only vegans.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Reveals the question bothers them.</bold></text><text> By feeling the need to make up for it.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph heading='2'><text>9. Biases</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Bias</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>How it sustains L27</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Evidence</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Psychological reactance</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Feeling one&#x27;s freedom threatened produces the opposite behavior</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>General literature on reactance</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Do-gooder derogation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Irritation with those who act morally</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://faculty.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2011/12/Minson---Monin,-2011.pdf">Minson and Monin, 2012</link></text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>10. Extrapolations</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The principle being tested is: &quot;if someone asks me not to do something, I may do more of it to provoke them&quot;.</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Applied to</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>What would follow</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Note</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Environmentalists</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Polluting more to provoke them would be acceptable</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>No one seriously defends this</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Anti-smoking activists</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Smoking more to provoke them</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Harms oneself</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Vegans</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Eating more meat to provoke them</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Harms the animals</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>11. What the argument gets right</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The phrase reveals something real: many people feel pressured or judged when the subject is meat, and react with humor or provocation. Animal advocates should understand this reaction and avoid feeding it.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>But provocation isn&#x27;t an answer. And when the irritation passes, the question about the animals is still there, waiting.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>References</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Psychology</bold></text></paragraph><list kind='bullet'><listItem><paragraph><text><link href="https://faculty.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2011/12/Minson---Monin,-2011.pdf">Minson, J. A., &amp; Monin, B. (2012). Do-gooder derogation. *Social Psychological and Personality Science*, 3(2)</link></text><text>.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text>Brehm, J. W. (1966). </text><text><italic>A Theory of Psychological Reactance</italic></text><text>.</text></paragraph></listItem></list></doc>